@ 15.2.6 - Proto-Eskimo to Nunivak Yup’ik
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Swadesh, Morris (1952), “Unaaliq and Proto Eskimo IV: Diachronic Notes”, International Journal of American Linguistics, Vol. 18, No. 3 (Jul., 1952), pp. 166–171 Regressive MOA and voicing assimilation in consonant clusters, at least when C₂ is either /l/ or /l̥/
	C:[+long] > C
	C > C:[+long] / _V(…V) except in #%
	S > ∅ / #_F
	s > t͡s / in certain situations?
	C:[+voice] > C:[-voice] / adjacent to {S,s,l̥}
	{ ɣ, ʁ } > { x, χ } / _#
	ə > a / _#
	ə > ∅
	i > ∅ / #C:[+dental]_C:[+dental]V
	a > ∅ / C:[+velar]_C:[+velar]
	{ v, ʁ } > { f, χ } / S_
	F:[+voice] > F:[-voice] / adjacent to {S,t͡s}
	F:[+voice] > S / l̥_
	t > s / :{ _k, _q }:
	{ i, a, u } > { ii, aa, uu } / C_ in %:[+open -initial -final] such that %:[+open]_
	ə > i / :{ u_ (though aə seems to have become i in some circumstances), a_ (though aə seems to have become i in some circumstances) }:
	v > ∅ / u:[+short]_V:[+short]
	v > ∅ / V:[+short]_u:[+short]
	u > ∅ / #_vV
	iv > j / #_u
	s > d͡ʒ / :{ i_V, u_V }:
	d͡ʒ > ∅ / i_i
	ə > ∅ / _d͡ʒ{a,u} except in #%
	a > ∅ / _d͡ʒa (except in #%?)
	in > d͡ʒ / _u (possibly only word-initially?)